trollers from retained histories. The final editor/session and chat/isolate diagrams develop these concepts effectively.","analysis explicitly distinguishes static call evidence from observed execution at seed lines 49–51, and separates dependency facts from partitions at lines 84–98. source lines 20–23 and 50–54, together with contexts lines 13–18, also preserve meaningful distinctions between content, range and descriptor hashes."],"issues":[{"evidence":"runtime seed lines 38–100 enumerate a large constructor/service graph, followed by endpoint and error inventories at lines 113–151. Its final central diagram still devotes lines 27–71 to service wiring and remains 105 lines tall. storage seed includes debounce/retry timings at lines 44–55, writer concurrency at line 81 and cache capacities at line 152; its final central diagram retains many of these details at lines 30–35, 51 and 97. source seed similarly includes cache capacity and rendering chunk size at lines 75 and 92.","impact":"Phase 2 gives incidental constants and collaborator lists nearly the same prominence as architectural decisions. Later compression therefore leaves large central trees whose main story competes with operational reference material. Later additions contribute further density, but the lack of prioritization is already present in the seeds.","recommendation":"Regenerate around the decisions each point explains. Keep details that change the reader's model—such as separate publication boundaries, failure recovery or lifetime limits—and compress constructor inventories and routine constants into their governing behavior. Retain exact limits selectively when their consequence is important, rather than treating every available implementation detail as presentation-worthy."},{"evidence":"generation repeats retained-output retry behavior in seed lines 26–30, 66–82, 84–94 and 112–119; final central lines 45–60 and 73–76 still repeat it, with further treatment in batch and lifetime. agent-api repeats mutation recovery across seed lines 42–46, 79–83 and 116–119, retained in final central lines 37–39, 57–60 and 79–81. editor repeats controller state ownership and publication under seed lines 7–15, 21–34, 53–60 and 77–89.","impact":"Separate structural, call, ownership and dependency passes often retell the same mechanism instead of adding a distinct insight. This creates apparent breadth while increasing reading and reconciliation effort. The final peripherals amplify this inherited repetition, although their exact duplication is a later editing choice.","recommendation":"Give each mechanism one primary explanation within its seed. Add another perspective only when it contributes a new relationship, prerequisite or consequence. For example, explain generation acknowledgement recovery once, then make the ownership branch identify only what retains the prepared result and when that retention ends. Choose organization independently for each point."},{"evidence":"The same handoffs receive substantial treatment in multiple seeds: diagrams lines 76–89 and generation lines 39–53 describe candidate submission and revision issuance; canvas lines 108–131 and storage lines 26–38 describe checkpoint records and revision dependencies; editor lines 67–75 and agent-api lines 51–54 describe browser save/publication. These overlaps persist in final diagrams/source lines 19–26, generation/validation lines 18–23, canvas/restore lines 25–31, storage/central lines 17–38 and editor/boundary lines 2–17. source and contexts also discuss related evidence with different hashes: source seed lines 20–23 and 36–37 versus contexts lines 13–18 and 102–107.","impact":"Individual canvases are reasonably self-contained, but the collection lacks a clear division between the authoritative explanation of a mechanism and a caller's view of it. Readers repeatedly encounter similar flows while having to infer whether a version, hash, revision or acknowledgement refers to the same object.","recommendation":"Coordinate the seeds as a collection. Assign each shared mechanism a primary explanatory point and retain concise caller/callee contracts elsewhere. At handoffs, name the transferred object and identity scope explicitly—for example, document version, diagram revision, workspace revision, descriptor hash or source fingerprint. Preserve local context without repeating the full downstream implementation."},{"evidence":"runtime seed lines 83–90 nest LayoutTemplateService, ReferenceGenerationService, JudgementService and ExperimentService beneath EvalRunService inside an ownership-labelled service graph; final runtime/eval lines 2–11 distinguish constructor inputs from collaborators assigned afterward. generation seed lines 54–59 place sequential variant generation under 'Install according to operation'; final generation/central lines 33–42 separate preparation from installation. changes seed line 32 presents 'runAgentWorkflow: analyze -> materialize -> validate', whereas final changes/central lines 20–21 and changes/run lines 11–16 distinguish workflow execution from subsequent service validation.","impact":"Tree nesting and arrows sometimes blur injected dependency, ownership, execution sequence and conceptual grouping. The final outputs explicitly repair several such ambiguities. This is a Phase 2 relationship-modelling problem, independent of later layout; the supplied outputs do not establish source-level errors beyond these differences in representation.","recommendation":"Regenerate these branches with explicit relationship verbs and correctly scoped stages. Use containment only where it communicates a real grouping or ownership relationship; identify constructor inputs, assigned collaborators and callbacks separately when that distinction matters. Keep preparation, invocation, validation and publication boundaries clear without requiring a fixed set of relationship categories."},{"evidence":"Several seed statements are broader than their final counterpart